Fixed. Your system pip works again.

**What was wrong:** the `pip/` package itself was missing from disk. `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/` still had `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` (metadata, RECORD listing every file) but not the files it described. So `pip`, `pip3` and `python3 -m pip` all died at startup with:

```
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'
```

Worth clearing out of the way: this is *not* PEP 668. The `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` file on this box belongs to Debian's Python 3.11 at `/usr/lib/python3.11/`; your `python3` is the official `python:3.13` image at `/usr/local/bin/python3`, which has no such file. No venv is needed to install system-wide here — the install was root-owned and you are root.

**The fix:** `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` does *not* repair this — it sees the surviving dist-info, decides pip is "already satisfied", and leaves you broken. The working route was unpacking the bundled wheel over the top:

```
cd /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages
python3 -m zipfile -e /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl .
```

**Verification** (run after the repair, independently of it):

```
pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)
DRY-RUN INSTALL OK
REAL INSTALL OK 1.17.0
```

`python3 -m pip install --target /tmp/realinstall six` fetched from PyPI, unpacked, and `import six` succeeded — a genuine end-to-end install, done with `--target` so your system site-packages were not touched by the test.

One leftover: I proposed a skill capturing this failure mode and its fix, so it isn't re-derived next time. It's sitting at `.gnomon/skills/proposed/repairing-broken-pip-in-the-python-3-13-image.md` and only becomes active if you run `gnomon skill accept repairing-broken-pip-in-the-python-3-13-image` — accepting changes the surface hash, which is a human decision, so I left it proposed.

